Carry the genuine message id through the one daemon -> module write seam.

Nothing today links a byte of the module's stdin to the message that caused
it.  output_send_data() receives only the payload, so the per message
TSpeechDMessage has to be threaded to it, and one record per write has to be
appended to a sideband file that the daemon itself creates.

Identity in the record is PRIMARY msg->id, the same value the client sees as
field 1 of every 7xx header and as the data line of the 225 reply, scoped by
the physical pipe (fstat of output->pipe_in[1]), by the birth of this daemon
and of this module instance, and by the ACTUAL byte extent that moved.
A birth is field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat alone, the one unsigned start
time in clock ticks; fields 3 to 21 are stepped over as whole whitespace
delimited tokens, because field 3 is a state letter and a signed field
such as tpgid holds a bare -1, and a dirty or wrapped field 22 yields no
birth at all instead of an invented one.
msg->settings.uid and msg->settings.fd travel as auxiliary scope only and
never attribute anything.  speaking_uid is never read.

Everything is off unless NOGUI_MODULE_OBSERVER_META names a file: then the
write path costs one load and one branch and stays byte identical to stock.
The metadata file is created once, exclusively, mode 0600, inside an owned
directory with no group or other bits, by this daemon and by no one else; the
same descriptor is kept for the whole daemon life, module reload only bumps
the epoch, module unload never closes it, and daemon shutdown releases it
exactly once.  An existing path is refused, never truncated, adopted or
unlinked.  A file this daemon did create in that path is removed again,
through the same verified directory descriptor, when the setup below it
fails, so a failed daemon leaves no empty file to refuse the next module.
A metadata failure is permanent, silent to the module, and never
changes a reply, a payload or a module status.

Apply after tools/patches/speech-dispatcher-write-all.patch, which this
extends: the record reports what output_write_all() actually wrote.

